        ":question/problem": "![](images/9b/9babf4c2ec631b3b872760261a46c3552cb93b93df90515ff0605c15043841ca.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=\u27e84\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9.$*",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\u27e84\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
